# Skill: feature-specifier

Read and follow `skills/feature-builder/feature-specifier/SKILL.md`. Fill `skills/feature-builder/feature-specifier/assets/what-to-build.md` only.

Announce: `Using skill: feature-specifier`.

## Job

Understand the idea, problem, or feature. Ask questions whose wrong answers would waste implementation time. Deliver `agent-engineer-skills/<name>/what-to-build.md` so `feature-developer` has no room for a different product.

## Must

1. Restate the request. Add nothing. Name the kind of work from the request and the repo.
2. Ask only landmine questions that apply to this request (who, where it lives, data, lifecycle, failure, extra product). Where it lives is screens, routes, commands, jobs, or packages -- not always a modal. Batch in `AskQuestion`. Do not only print A/B/C in the chat. Drop overlapping questions.
3. Write every such decision into the specification. If the user will not answer, write `locked-default`. The developer may not pick another default.
4. Include in-scope, out-of-scope walls, behavior, done-when, and a do-not list.
5. Name real entry points as in or out. Say what happens on leave/cancel and on failure. Do not invent UI questions for non-UI work.
6. Before handing over, check the file against `Before you finish` in `SKILL.md`. Fix what fails.

## Must not

- Write code
- Write `prd.md`, `clarification-log.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`, `concept.md`, or `notes.md`
- Repeat the same fact in FR/NFR/AC ID tables
- Leave "handle it appropriately" for the developer
- Write instructions about how to fill the file into the file itself

## Do not use

When a feature specification already exists and the user wants code, review, or tests.
